    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel disappointed and to scrutinize every detail of the process after coming so close to a role—especially with 15 years of experience, five of them as deputy news editor. \n\nHowever, to answer your questions directly: **no, you should not make something of the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women, and on its own, it is not a sign of bias or unfair practice.**\n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why, along with practical advice on how to handle the situation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition on Its Own Is Not Evidence of Bias\n* **Operational reality:** At public broadcasters and large media organizations, panels usually comprise specific role-holders—for example, the Head of News, an HR partner, a Digital Director, and another senior editor. If those positions happen to be held by women, the panel will naturally be all-female. \n* **Standard of proof:** Just as an all-male panel does not inherently prove bias against female candidates, an all-female panel does not prove bias against male candidates. Raising gender without concrete, documented evidence of discrimination (such as inappropriate questions or overt bias during the interview) will almost certainly be viewed as unfounded grievance and could seriously damage your professional reputation within the organization and the industry.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experience vs. Strategic Priority\n* **Tenure vs. specialized skills:** While 15 years in news and five years as deputy is a strong track record, tenure alone does not guarantee an appointment. Modern public broadcasters are facing immense pressure regarding digital transformation, changing audience habits, and social/online reach. \n* **The role's mandate:** If the announcement specifically highlighted her \"strong experience of digital media,\" that suggests the station prioritized digital reinvention over traditional broadcast experience or longer tenure. \n* **Interview performance & vision:** Shortlisted candidates are usually all qualified on paper. The final decision often comes down to the editorial vision presented, strategic responses to hypothetical news crises, or leadership style.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay for Feedback\n* Two weeks without a written response is very common in public sector and public broadcast environments. Written feedback usually requires HR sign-off, internal documentation, and often does not get finalized until the successful candidate has formally signed their contract.\n* A delay of two weeks is an administrative lag, not necessarily a sign of bad faith or evasion.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up:**  \n   Reach out to HR or the panel chair with a courteous note. Frame your request around professional development rather than challenging the outcome:\n   > *\"I hope you are well. I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the news editor interview. As someone deeply committed to the organization and looking to continue developing my editorial leadership, any specific insights or areas for growth the panel could share would be invaluable.\"*\n\n2. **Listen closely to the feedback when it arrives:**  \n   Pay attention to specific areas where the panel felt the other candidate excelled (e.g., digital workflows, audience engagement metrics, multimedia strategy). This will give you a clear roadmap of what skills or projects to focus on for future opportunities.\n\n3. **Focus on building the identified gap:**  \n   If digital strategy was indeed the differentiator, look for opportunities to lead cross-platform or digital-first projects in your current role to bolster your portfolio for the next editorial opening.\n",
